A handoff that lists what exists but not what misleads sends the next agent into
the same holes. Three files at the root look current and are not.

`nb` is a repository-maintenance script from earlier in this project, now
superseded by the explicit `git am` flow. It cost four rounds of debugging —
`git am` returns inconsistently on an already-applied patch, and `git checkout
main` fails while a tracked file is modified, which its fallback then made worse
by trying to create a branch that already existed. The next agent will find it,
try it, and lose the same afternoon. Now it says so.

The two `0001-*.patch` files are superseded by `0002-baseline-patches-001-and-002`,
which is the applied result of both plus the patch-002 script; applying either
0001 fails or duplicates. The chain starts at 0002.

`patch-002-remove-fabricated-compliance.py` is historical but worth reading once:
it is the commit that deleted fabricated SBOM hashes and a hardcoded
"integrity": "hash_chain_verified" literal, and it is the origin of the rule the
whole codebase runs on.

All three are documented rather than deleted, with the one-line command to
remove them, because they are the operator's files and quietly deleting somebody
else's work is the same species of unhelpfulness as `git clean` eating a patch
that was uploaded ten seconds earlier.

Also corrects the header: 16 assurance commits became 17 when HANDOFF.md itself
was committed. In a document whose whole claim is that every number in it is
checkable, a stale self-reference is the one error that costs the most.

